        {
            "texttype":"Online",
            "textpurpose":"Gallery Label",
            "textentryhtml":"<p>\n\tNumerous mass-produced, mold-made elements compose this assemblage sculpture. The central scene, framed by a tablero platform and an angled roof, indicates an architectural setting in distinctively Teotihuacan style. An owl perches at the top of the censer while birds and severed arms hang from the eaves above a pair of seated human ﬁgures. Three additional ﬁgures, two wearing dog or coyote masks, accompany them. The upper portion of the censer could be lifted off to place copal (pine-tree resin) or rubber incense within, the smoke from which would emanate from the tubular shaft that runs through the center of the scene.\n</p>\n<p>\n\tThe overall form and the style of the ﬁgures derive from the Classic-period metropolis of Teotihuacan. However, this censer and many similar ceramic works were produced along the Paciﬁc piedmont of Guatemala, possibly by members of a migrant community from the distant city.\n</p>",
            "remarks":"AAA3_20-T6-3_CLA_FA.pdf  - Day 1 installation"
        }
    ],
